Close the chown(2)/fchownat(2) existence oracle.

  chown(p, -1, -1)
  fchownat(AT_FDCWD, p, -1, -1, 0)

is the same SECOND mechanism ghost_chmod.patch closes, on the sibling syscall
that was not written at the time:

  hidden injected path   -> EROFS
  genuinely absent path  -> ENOENT
  stock visible file     -> EROFS

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT

do_fchownat() takes mnt_want_write() on the read-only ROM mount immediately
after user_path_at() returns and before chown_common() runs, so it answers
-EROFS before anything re-validates that the name exists -- the hijacked
->setattr and ->permission are never consulted. Byte-for-byte the ordering
ghost_chmod.patch documents for do_fchmodat(). Verified against v6.12 fs/open.c
and against all five pinned OnePlus trees.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Immediately after user_path_at() returns success, before mnt_want_write(). That
is AFTER the lookup -- so the parent directory's search permission has already
been enforced, and a caller who cannot search it still gets -EACCES exactly as
it would for an absent name -- and BEFORE the write reference that produces the
EROFS. Read the note in ghost_o_path.patch on why this is the opposite
placement rule from common/_hook/ before moving it.

The error path is the function's own `goto out_release`, which path_put()s the
path exactly once and then runs the retry_estale() check; -ENOENT is not
-ESTALE, so the retry loop is not re-entered.

APPLIES TO

do_fchownat() is BYTE-IDENTICAL on all five fleet kernel versions -- md5 of the
extracted function body matches across v5.10, v5.15, v6.1, v6.6 and v6.12 -- so
one file covers them, and unlike chmod there is no second shape to fall back to.

Dry-run at -F0 against the pinned OnePlus trees for 5.10, 5.15, 6.1, 6.6 and
6.12: applies to all five, offsets only. Generated by editing the real file and
running `diff -up`, never by hand.

--- a/fs/open.c
+++ b/fs/open.c
@@ -819,6 +819,21 @@ retry:
 	error = user_path_at(dfd, filename, lookup_flags, &path);
 	if (error)
 		goto out;
+	{
+		extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
+
+		/*
+		 * mnt_want_write() below answers -EROFS on a read-only ROM
+		 * mount before anything re-validates that the name exists, so
+		 * the engine's hijacked ->setattr and ->permission never get a
+		 * turn -- exactly the ordering ghost_chmod.patch documents for
+		 * do_fchmodat(). An absent name gives -ENOENT here.
+		 */
+		if (ghost_hidden_path(&path)) {
+			error = -ENOENT;
+			goto out_release;
+		}
+	}
 	error = mnt_want_write(path.mnt);
 	if (error)
 		goto out_release;
